        "summary":  "A Workato survey of IT executives reveals that nearly two-thirds anticipate agentic AI security incidents within 18 months, driven by rapid agent deployment and rising shadow AI adoption. Roughly one-third of leaders indicated they would attempt to handle major AI security breaches quietly without public disclosure, reflecting weak governance frameworks. The findings highlight that agent security risk is being treated as an inevitability rather than a preventable outcome.",
        "execSummary":  "Enterprise AI security is entering a new risk tier: agents that act autonomously on behalf of the business create a breach surface that traditional access-control models were not designed for. The finding that a third of leaders would suppress disclosure of major AI incidents is the governance red flag — it signals that accountability structures for agents are not yet operationalized. For customer-support leaders deploying agents with access to customer data, case history, and fulfillment systems, the immediate action is defining the agent\u0027s permission boundary, audit trail, and incident response protocol before deployment, not after the first incident. Regulators will not accept \u0027the agent did it\u0027 as an accountability answer.",
