        "date":  "2026-10-15",
        "theme":  "Agents",
        "summary":  "The article argues that AI agents require access to authoritative systems of record — not just memory — to safely execute consequential actions in enterprise environments. Memory tells an agent what happened; it does not confirm what is true in the current system state, creating risk of duplicate actions, incorrect assumptions, and unsafe operations. The author outlines patterns for wiring agents to verifiable state stores, including Temporal, AWS Step Functions, and Azure Durable Functions.",
        "execSummary":  "The gap between what an AI agent remembers and what the underlying system actually reflects is the primary source of agent reliability failures in production. For customer-support leaders deploying agents that perform transactions — refunds, ticket updates, account changes — this is the architectural requirement that cannot be skipped: the agent must verify system state before acting, not rely on conversation history. The practical implication is that agent design must include explicit state-verification checkpoints wired to systems of record, and that support platforms must expose reliable state-query APIs for agents to consume. This article provides the engineering vocabulary for making that requirement concrete in vendor and engineering conversations.",
