        "theme":  "Philosophy",
        "summary":  "As autonomous AI agents take on independent enterprise work, organizations must move beyond policy documents to establish technical accountability frameworks. The article argues that agent accountability requires definable agent identity, enforced permission boundaries, traceable delegation of authority through sub-agents, and system-level lifecycle ownership. Without these technical controls, accountability exists only on paper and cannot be audited or enforced.",
        "execSummary":  "The accountability question for AI agents is not philosophical — it is an engineering and governance requirement that enterprise AI programs must resolve before scaling. The article\u0027s core argument is that accountability must be enforced at the system level, not assumed from policy: an agent without a verifiable identity, bounded permissions, and traceable delegation chain is an unauditable actor in enterprise workflows. For customer-support leaders, this frames the minimum viable governance bar for any agent with access to customer data or the ability to take actions on behalf of the business. Organizations that cannot answer \u0027which agent did this, under whose authority, with what permissions\u0027 are exposed to both regulatory and operational risk as agent autonomy increases.",
